From caf-talk Caf Apr 12 09:29:03 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: AIA: Credit Where None is Due (fwd)
Message-ID: <1993Apr12.132851.1274@eff.org>
Date: Mon, 12 Apr 1993 13:28:51 GMT

[Posted by request of Jim Thomas (tk0jut1@niu.bitnet) - Carl]

John Hinton's recent post (omitted here) alluded to a study done by
the NEH in 1989. He used the findings, which concluded that U.S.
students lack basic knowledge in history, literature, and other
humanities disciplines, to raise the new right's complaints against
what these critics see as the consequences of "liberal" education.
Hinton's argument is specious:

1) The survey is old news, and was well publicized at the time.  The
findings were no surprise to anybody, including the targets of
"Accuracy in Academia," who have been complaining of this for many
years.

2) Part of the problem is that students generally stay away from the
humanities. Especially during the 1980s, market incentives discouraged
majoring in humanities or in taking elective humanities courses that
would not be marketable or that would prolong students' graduation
dates. During my 12 years as an academic advisor, I also found
students tended to avoid humanities because it wasn't "relevant" to
their lives.

3) Adults, presumably products of the type of education that AIA feels
has been subverted by the "new liberalism," do little if any better on
cultural knowledge. This is significant because it indicates that as a
society we simply do not have a particularly strong interest in the
type of knowledge measure by the NEH survey.

AIA's political agenda includes adducing any evidence it can find,
even if off-mark, to rail against multi-culturalism as the cause of
current problems in education.  AIA and its supporters fail to
recognize that the problems of contemporary education, and there are
many, are grounded in rapid social, political, and economic forces
that cannot be resolved by falsely placing the blame at the feet of
"tenured radicals." Their continued claim that the "cultural elite"
would replace, rather than supplement, traditional humanities with
non-Western courses is wrong (the Stanford curriculum reform is one
example of how these critics distort facts).

Few "untenured radicals" support removing basic courses in Western
culture. In a time of fiscal crunch, created in part by 12 years of
Reagan/Bush inattentiveness to education, humanities departments
especially are being forced to cut back by reducing faculty size,
limiting course offerings, and restricting enrollments. A variety of
barriers discourage students from pursuing the humanities. There are
fewer academic jobs for humanities Phds, reducing the pool of
competent future teachers.

Contrary to AIA's claims, "tenured radicals" simply do not control
the universities, let alone the humanities. Universities are
controlled by bureaucrats who make decisions on the basis of
perceived organizational exigencies. They may goof (and goof badly)
on occasion, but their agenda is hardly dictated by some
nebulous leftist agenda as AIA would have us believe.

Jim Thomas



