From caf-talk Caf Apr 12 12:25:26 1993
Newsgroups: news.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: "Dear SysAdmin, Punish your user for what he said!"
Message-ID: <1993Apr12.162516.4665@eff.org>
Date: Mon, 12 Apr 1993 16:25:16 GMT


>Summary: Please send samples of how you respond to such requests.

[From a state university - Carl]

============================
(1) I look at how I was asked.  That says a lot right there.
(2) I look at what our user originally posted.
(3) I talk it over with the associate director of academic computing, 
    since I'm a technical person, rather than a formal policy-maker.
(4) If it actually requires some sort of action, the action is usually
    a forged cancel to reduce the damage, and a "don't do that any more"
    e-mail.

We've never (knock on wood) had major problems, like chain letters, robo-
posters, "fags must die", etc. posters from our university system.

Now, on the community access system (XXXXXXXX) we've had to nuke
no more than 20 accounts in five years.  These were people that were repeatedly
advised in e-mail, on the phone, etc. that the disruption they were causing
was unacceptable.  These were all in local discussion forums, in e-mail,
or on our local chat-server.  I can only name two, so 20 is probably a very
high estimate.

