From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: When is it illegal to remove 'alt.sex'?
Message-ID: <1993Apr12.203152.10723@eff.org>
Date: Mon, 12 Apr 1993 20:31:52 GMT

Here are some excerpts and notes from chapter 3, "Sex and School
Library" of _Sex, Schools and the Law_, by Fernand N. Dutile
(Professor of Law, U. of Notre Dame), 1986, Charles C. Thomas
Publisher, Springfield Illinois_. It is about _Island Tree_ a Supreme
Court case about removing books for a high school library.

WHAT HAPPENED:

"In September of 1975, three member of the Board of Education of the
Island Trees Union Free School District No. 26, in New York, attended
a conference sponsored by a politically conservative group of parents
[...] dealing with education. At the conference, they obtained lists
of books called 'objectionable' by one of these three and 'improper
fare for school students' by another. [...] In February of 1976, the
Board issued an 'unofficial directive' that the listed library books
be taken to the Board's office so that the Board members might read
them. After the books were removed, the directive became generally
known and the Board issued a press release justifying its action and
characterizing the books involved as 'anti-American, anti-Christian,
anti-Semitic, and just plain filthy.' The release concluded with a
reference to the Board's perceived moral obligation to protect school
children from 'moral danger.'"

The Board appointed a committee of parents and staff members to make
recommendations. The committee recommend that five books be retained
and two removed. It could not agree about two others.

The Board "substantially rejected" the report and removed all nine books.

Two students sued.

U.S. District Court granted summary judgment in favor of the School Board.

"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit disagreed, finding a
trial necessary to resolve whether removal was politically motivated
and whether the books were removed because of the ideas they
contained."

"The United States Supreme Court affirmed the need for a trial, on a
five-to-four vote, but no clear theory of the merits of the case
controlled the majority of the Court. Justice Brennan, joined by
Justices Marshal and Stevens, emphasized the student's right to
recieve information and concluded that a school board may not remove
books from the school libraries merely because it dislikes the idea in
those books and seeks to proscribe by such removal political,
religions, or other orthodoxy. Concurring in most of Justice Brennan's
approach, but embracing 'a somewhat different perspective,' Justice
Blackmum observed that the state may not 'suppress exposure to ideas
-- for the sole _purpose_ of suppression exposure of those ideas --
absent sufficiently compelling reason." Justice White felt it was
premature to discuss limits on school board discretion prior to
resolution of the factual issues at the trail. Justice Burger, Powell,
Rehnquist and O'Connor, dissenting, felt that the school board should
prevail on the pleading, with a trial."

No trial ensured because the Board decided to restore the books.

REMOVAL FACTORS

"[Other] cases do make clear that school library book decision may be
based on neutral factors, that is, those unconnected with content.
Among these are space considerations {142}, financial factors {143},
and the physical condition of the book }144},. School library book
decision presumably may also reflect factors which though related to
content, are politically neutral, such as obsolescence {145},
relevance {146}, psychological or intellectual appropriateness for a
particular age groups {147}, curricular priorities {148}, duplication
of resources {149} and balance of views {150}."


========
References

Board of Education, Island Trees Union Free School District No. 26 v.
Pico, 457 U.S.854-861 (1982)

For the removal factor references see p. 97 of Dutile.

- Carl
