> > 9.  (Carl Kadie, summarized): "Sued on what grounds? Most sexual
> > material is constitutionally protected. 'Harmful to minor laws'
> > only apply to legally obscene material. Moreover, most such laws
> > explicitly exempt libraries and universities. Finally, since the
> > university was acting like a bookstore or library and not a
> > publisher, it seems unlikely that it would be held liable for
> > access even to obscene material." Annotated references follow.

Brad Hicks writes:

>I hope that nobody depends on this for legal advice, because Carl, if what
>I've read is right, you are very definitely wrong.

I got this information from the 1992 ACLU Handbook _The Rights of
Authors, Artists, and Other Creative People_. It is consistent with an
editorial in the Chicago Tribune about a month ago about a library
book removal. Since I posted originally the information a few weeks
ago, I've asked three lawyers about it. An Illinois ACLU lawyer said
it was correct.

On the other hand, Lance Rose and Mike Godwin both said it was
incorrect. They pointed to _Dial Information vs. Barr_ (which allowed
the FCC to restrict nonobscene dial-a-sex services). Lance said he
thought there were other lower court cases, too, but didn't know them
off hand.

So, .... now I don't know. I would love to hear more about specific cases.

>  According to an article I just read in the newsletter of the ACLU
>of Eastern Missouri, (1) Harmful to minors laws apply just as
>strongly to "indecent" material as they do to obscene materials.

We'll I'm sure many of the the laws are written that way. The question
is can they be constitionally applied to material with serious
literary, artistic, political, or scientific value?

>They'd have to, wouldn't they?  After all, obscene materials are
>illegal to distribute to adults, so of course they're illegal to
>distribute to minors, even without seperate laws!

Not necessarily. First, obscene materials are not automatically
illegal.  They are illegal only if a law makes them illegal. Second,
the definition of obscenity-for-an-adult could be different than
obscenity-for-an-minor because a jury might find that material appeals
to the prurient interest of a minor that doesn't appeal to the
prurient interest of an adult.

>  (2) Do you have a reference for the claim that =most= state and
>local laws exempt libraries and universities?  I suspect "some" is
>more accurate.

You are probably right. I know Iowa does. I know Illinois used to, but
no longer does.

>And (3) BBS owners have been held liable for allow minors access to
>non-obscene indecent material; in fact, a sysop here in St. Louis just
>lost $40k worth of hardware over just such an allegation.

An allegation is not a conviction. (At least it is not suppose to be.)

- Carl

