 WHAT YOU WON'T GET AT GROVE CITY COLLEGE  By Michael L. Coulter

  Every choice involves a limitation.  By choosing to go to Grove
 City College, you have chosen not to go to another college.  Any
 GCC student could have gone elsewhere, perhaps even an Ivy League
 school.  What are students at _top_ schools getting that students
 at GCC aren_t?
  This past summer I participated in an internship program with
 students from Princeton, Yale, Harvard, Brown, Stanford, the
 University of Michigan, the University of Chicago_institutions of
 higher learning often referred to as the best in the country.  We
 had great opportunity to talk about our respective colleges and
 universities.  Obviously, there is great variance between GCC and
 the aforementioned schools.  Admittedly, these schools have better
 research facilities and I would imagine a better cafeteria (but
 they also pay between two and three times what we pay).
  Probably the most salient difference, however, was their
 conspicuous presence of radical intellectual life.  There is a
 current of social and intellectual radicalism flowing in American
 higher education.  Contrary to current jargon, radical does not
 mean something that is different or _cool._  The radicalism to
 which I refer is a systematic undermining of the values,
 documents, and institutions which compose Western civilization.
 It is present in the faculty, mostly the humanities and social
 sciences, and in the student body.  In many of these institutions,
 the administration has succumbed to the radical elements of their
 schools.
  At Yale, it is referred to as being _P.C._ (politically correct)
 to support and participate in Gay and Lesbian Awareness Week.  At
 the University of Michigan, a speech code was enacted(it has since
 been struck down in court as a violation of free speech) that
 prohibited _racist, sexist, and homophobic language._  A
 University of Michigan student was reprimanded because he
 complained about a gay and lesbian table located near a cafeteria.
 The gay and lesbian student association at Harvard is lobbying for
 Gay Studies to be a major.
  At Brown, Harvard, and Yale universities one can major in Women_s
 or Black Studies.  The leitmotif of the feminists and militant
 blacks who teach these courses is that Western civilization is the
