[From a large state university - Carl]

===========================
Whew.  This tends to open up a *big* can of worms.  If it's just one or two
users who complain, I usually respond politely, but it doesn't seem to me that
one or two complaints warrants much action.  With so many users on Usenet,
*anyone* is bound to tick off *someone*.  Rude complaints usually get a firm
"Please don't bother me again" type response.

Lots of complaints from various users usually warrant some investigation.
There are several question I have to consider:

   (1)	Are they abusing the network?

	For example, one of those immense crossposts to a bazillion
	inappropriate groups, or a commercial post in an inappropriate place.
	The user gets *one* warning.  A second offense usually results in
	removal of posting privs, which can be appealed.  A third offense, and
	I nuke 'em---I don't need users like that.

   (2)	Are they just being inflammatory?

	If so, what sort of "inflammatory" are we talking about?  I usually
	wind up talking with them to find out what they're up to.  If they're
	just trying to infuriate people, they get a warning.  Another post, and
	they get a final warning.  Next time, revocation of their posting
	privs, which may be appealed.  Another offense, and I nuke 'em---if
	they're really using my machine for something important, I might be
	able to let 'em stay on, but with a *clear* understanding that Usenet
	is off-bounds.  I don't intend to support the kind of crap and noise
	that Usenet is *already* swamped with.

   (3)	Are they just being . . . well, *weird*?

	Depending on the newsgroup(s) they're doing this in, this may fall
	under (2) above.  Robert McElwane, for example, would not be on my
	system for long.

Note:	In general, I don't *care* what my users post.  When it starts
	reflecting badly on my site, though, I start thinking about putting my
	foot down.  When the user Doesn't Get It, on a chronic basis, I *do*
	put my foot down.
==========================


