I wrote the following in reply to a post to this list by Liz Stokes, but due
to some glitch in the reply function, it initially went to her instead of to
CAF-Talk.
                           *    *    *

Liz Stokes wrote:
>
[stuff deleted]
>	I repeat - it's a public forum. You have to take responsibility for
>anything you say in public. That professor may be filtering the group to find
>his students' postings, or someone else might bring it to his attention. We're
>not talking about private email here - it something you've just shipped out to
>11 thousand systems. Usenet might be a more civil place if folks thought people
>they know personally might read what they wrote.

Somehow, all this reminds me of the dilemma of those who would publish their
ideas in the non-electronic media, but would then be vulnerable to retribution
by whomever.  This was an especially common dilemma during the golden age :-)
of McCarthyism, but it remains a going concern to this day.  It's never a desi-
rable situation when there's a chilling effect on the good-faith expression of
ideas, as a result of the potential of anyone with the power to do so to make
life miserable, in a practical sense or otherwise, for the expressor.

A saying comes to mind, which I am told was a favorite of intellectuals and
dissidents in the pre-Glasnost USSR:  "Don't think.  If you think, don't speak.
If you speak, don't write.  If you write, don't publish...."

                              *    *    *

Liz then wrote back to me, as follows:

>   You have a point of course, people have suffered a great deal for
>what they have published -the obvious recent modern example comes to mind.
>Nonetheless, that doesn't make what was suggested blackmail. The original
>poster suggested forwarding a particulary vitriolic user's postings to his
>professors (it may not even have been a serious suggestions). Since there
>aren't any secrets, there can be no blackmail - you may not like it but you
>have to call it something else.

I had removed the excerpts from earlier postings which alluded to real or im-
agined blackmail.  My reference to "good-faith expression of ideas" was meant
to differentiate what I had in mind from what Liz, and the earlier posters,
apparently had in mind.  I still submit that there need not be "secrets" or
"blackmail" involved for a person's expressions, when placed in particular
hands, to lead to negative outcomes for that person.  Ideas expressed in good
faith are just as much at risk of this as the scurrilous expressions to which
other contributors were referring; it seems to me that the example somebody
cited--bringing an individual's posts to soc.motss to the attention of homo-
phobes in positions of authority--is an excellent example of this.

Liz and I may well be talking past each other on all this, but anyway... :-)
