In article <1993Apr12.151036.2895@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

-->  I am told that academic sys admins regularly receive demands to punish
-->  their users for something posted to Netnews. If you are a sys admin,
-->  how do you respond? ...  Please post your personal policy and, ...

  In all the years I've run USENET here, I've probably had two or three cases
of this happening (people complaining about my users).

  One was a person who posted a lot of (the same) articles without cross-
posting, which irritated someone.  I gave the original poster a few tips
on where and how to crosspost and that problem never came back.

  One was a user who persistently configured his mac newsreader incorrectly
and I took away "group" posting access.  This was a FastPath with dynamic
addressing, so it was impossible to figure out who was connected to what.
It's sort of debatable if they were trying to be obnoxious and anonymous
intentionally, but a combination of static addressing, properly configured
newsreader and the feeling that people "cared" (: solved _that_ problem.

  One user ran afoul of a venomous net-individual.  The user appeared quite
innocent and I said so.  It made an excellent example on why some people
should be ignored, although I've since lost the message.

  One user was posting "offensive" (to others) articles in something like
alt.sex.movies.  I believe the part they found offensive was that they
were movies where people were raped along with the language and attitude.
They were talking about movies, someone else had asked the question and
my user was only replying, and it seemed to be well within the charter so
I failed to find any real basis for a complaint and dismissed it.


  I think a little bit of watchfulness really goes a long ways towards
preventing problems.  You can't please everyone all the time, and USENET
is a whole lot of "everyone."  (:{

  If I ever really did have a problem, I'd probably approach it a few
ways.  Forging and what-not is easy to discipline for, as long as it's
not confused with improperly configured news software.  I think I'd try
to tackle most other topics under group charters...  IE, what the group
was created to discuss.  Nobody has ever really tried to make new policy
the hard way here.  (:

