I posed the question to the Sys Admin Guild ethics mailing list
(sage-ethics, listserv@usenix.org). Here are the replies:

From a computer company:

> My answer has always been something like:

> 	I think they have been punished enough having to work here.  :-)

From A private research company:

> Our policy is very simple: This is not a system administration
> problem. This is a management problem. We send the complaint on to the
> user's manager, and let them deal with it. (That is absolutely all we
> do about it - we don't even reply to the message except to say that we
> have forwarded it.) This of course does not work for students, who
> don't have managers.

> Does it work? Well, no. Nothing *works*. By the point where people get
> to sending this sort of e-mail, they are going to fight nastily no
> matter what you do. Fighting a flamewar is like dealing with burning
> magnesium; almost anything you do will make it burn hotter, and the
> best thing to do is get well out of the way and wait for it to burn
> down. If you absolutely have to fight it, purely overwhelming amounts
> of water help, but you have to be able to be *really* *serious* about
> it. What handing it to management does is a) get me out of the line of
> fire and b) give the problem to someone with bigger weapons at their
> disposal.

A follow up from a civilian government lab:

> > Our policy is very simple: This is not a system administration
> > problem. This is a management problem. We send the complaint on to the
> > user's manager, and let them deal with it. (That is absolutely all we
> > do about it - we don't even reply to the message except to say that we
> > have forwarded it.) This of course does not work for students, who
> > don't have managers.

> Curious. Do you also explain the concept of "NetNews" to your manager(s)? 

> > Does it work? Well, no. Nothing *works*. 

> I've found one thing that works _real_ well (it may not work for you):

> "USENET is interacted with at every reader's own risk. We take no
> responsibility for the validity of anything posted or read from this site.
> If you find a posting from this site offensive, please ignore it. If you
> don't know how to ignore a posting, complain to us and we will demonstrate."

> Putting your head in the sand like this may seem to be non-confrontive
> at best, until you realize the true level of triviality of articles people
> post. I don't think _I'd_ be willing to bug a manger with this kind of
> nonsense. 

[From a computer company

> I agree with [X from the private research lab] that this is not a
> System Administration issue, but perhaps for a different reason.  My
> view is that as a Sys Admin my job is to make sure that
> news/email/whatever data gets where it's supposed to go, it is not my
> job to legislate content even if it were technically feasable for me
> to do so (which it isn't).  I prefer to think of it as a
> common-carrier analogy: it's the phone company's business to make sure
> the obscene phone caller gets connected to your phone number when he
> dials it, but it's not their job to hunt him down and prosecute/punish
> him when it harasses you.

> Now it may be that a news post violates your institution's workplace
> harassment rules or equal opportunity statement.  In that case, the
> instution may choose to take action against the individual.  Though
> the System Administrator may be called upon as a technical expert, it
> is not within their domain to pursue such action on their own.  I also
> doubt there would be grounds to punish the person simply because
> somebody from another site complained-- though such complaints may
> prompt an internal investigation.

> To answer Carl's original question, I would prepare (_in advance_) a
> form letter for dealing with such situations.  The text should be
> cleared with your institution's HR or Legal Dept as appropriate, and
> should include appropriate contact information so that the
> complainants can forward their mail to somebody with more authority in
