We've never punished any user for expressing an opinion.  We have taken
action against users who have "letter bombed" or disrupted news groups in
an "uncivilized" manner.  However, those who have "disrupted" newsgroups
in a civilized manner have never been punished.

For example, I received a complaint via CERT about a particular posting in a
French language mailing list once that made a fairly good "test case".
I had just taken over the "net news" job and this was my first contact with
such problems.

CERT told me that someone on the list was "offended" by what was posted.
However, after translating the message into English, it sounded exactly like
a heated political debate, with "my" user commenting: "You should all be sat
in front of cannons, and I'll piss on your graves".  The debate involved the
topic of whether French or English should be the official language of Canada.

Needless to say I informed CERT that I would not get involved.  They
insisted that their complaintant was "offended".  I assured them that it was
his or her God given right to be offended.

But, to answer you question directly, we often reply to such complaints that
we will review the situation and take "appropriate action".
